//! The words of the page, for a reader with new eyes: one who may know
//! neither geometry nor machine learning nor X_eTaL. Each word of the
//! glossary to come (a manifold, intrinsic and ambient dimension,
//! locally flat, a geodesic, a data manifold, a polytope that is not a
//! manifold) is explained in plain words where it first appears. No
//! number lives here: the numbers on the page are the run's.

pub const HINT: &str = "This page is read from the top; every picture of a surface can be turned by dragging it, with the arrow keys once it has the focus, or with a finger. Turning changes no number.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 4] = [
    "What is a manifold, and why would a machine-learning experiment care?",
    "A list of numbers is a point. Three numbers are a point in a room; sixty-four numbers are a point in a room with sixty-four directions, which no one can picture but every program can hold. Machine learning lives on such points: a word, an image, a sound, each becomes a long list of numbers, and the model's work is to measure which points are near which.",
    "Near by what rule? The obvious rule is the straight line: square each difference, add them up, take the square root. This page is about the cases where that rule misleads, because the points do not fill their room. They lie on a curved thing inside it, a manifold, and along that thing the distances are different.",
    "Five of these curved things are drawn below, each from coordinates an X_eTaL program computed, and on each one measurement is made: two points near each other, two points far apart, and for each pair the straight line through the room against the distance along the thing. The near pairs nearly agree. The far pairs do not. That difference is the whole idea.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 5] = [
    "Three things are enough to read this page.",
    "A point is a list of numbers, and the count of numbers is the dimension of the room it sits in. A point of the plane has two numbers; a point of a room has three.",
    "The straight-line distance between two points is Pythagoras: the differences of their numbers, squared, added, and the square root taken. It is the length of the string pulled tight between them, through whatever lies between.",
    "A surface, like the skin of a ball, has fewer directions to move in than the room it sits in: on the ball's skin you can go north or east, two directions, inside a room of three. A tiny ant on the skin cannot tell it from a floor. That is the fact everything below rests on.",
    "Nothing on this page is trained, and nothing is random. The program computes points, lines and distances; the page draws them and quotes them.",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 6] = [
    "A manifold is a space that is locally flat: look at a small enough piece of it and it is, as near as you like, an ordinary flat space of some dimension. The earth is round, and the floor is flat, and both are true: the floor is a small piece of the earth. A circle is a manifold of one dimension (a small piece of it is nearly a straight line); the skin of a ball, a manifold of two. The number of directions you can move in along the manifold is its intrinsic dimension. The number of coordinates of the room it is drawn in is its ambient dimension. The two need not agree, and when they differ the manifold is curved inside its room.",
    "Locally flat has a consequence that can be measured, and the page measures it: between two points close together, the straight line through the room and the shortest path along the manifold are nearly the same length. Between two points far apart they are not: the string pulled tight cuts through the ball, and the path along the skin is longer. The shortest path along a manifold is a geodesic; on a ball it is an arc of a great circle, the route an airplane flies.",
    "Why would a machine-learning experiment care? Four reasons, each a track of this repository.",
    "Data: the points a model learns from are often near a curved thing of low intrinsic dimension inside a room of high ambient dimension (the manifold assumption: an assumption, not a fact, and the Swiss roll below is its classic picture). On it, the nearest point through the room can be the wrong neighbor. A representation: a model can be made to put its points on a chosen manifold, the sphere or the hyperbolic disk, and the choice changes what is near what. An optimizer: a step of training is a step through a parameter space, and the rule for the size of a step is a metric, a choice of distance; a step held to a manifold stays where the constraint wants it. A constraint: the set of allowed parameters (the mixing matrices of a residual stream, say) is a curved set of its own, and some such sets are smooth manifolds and some, like a polytope, are not: a polytope has corners and faces, it is flat in pieces but not locally flat at a corner, and this repository keeps the two words apart.",
    "This page measures the first idea only, on five manifolds. The experiments on the four tracks are the pages after it.",
    "The words introduced here, in the order they came: manifold, intrinsic dimension, ambient dimension, locally flat, geodesic, the manifold assumption and the data manifold, polytope. They are the first rows of the glossary this repository is writing.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 4] = [
    "This page is an introduction and a proof of a chain. It introduces what a manifold is on five of them, and it proves that a page here can run an X_eTaL program in the browser, read every coordinate back and draw it turning. Every number on the page, every point of every line, was computed by the program shown at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened. The page's own code only lays the numbers out, turns the pictures when asked, and quotes the results.",
    "It is not an experiment. It compares no models, trains nothing, and measures nothing but distances: for each manifold, the straight line against the way along, for a near pair and a far pair of points. The pages that compare geometries on a learning problem come after it (see What is next).",
    "The figures are marked. A measured figure is a distance the program computed between points it computed. An analytical figure is a shape drawn from its formula: the wire lines of a sphere are analytical, they show where the sphere is, not something learned; their points were still computed by the program, not by the page.",
    "The picture that turns is a plain orthographic projection: the same points seen from another side, nothing nearer drawn bigger. It is the page's own, a stopgap until the shared turning view of the X_eTaL-ML repository lands (ask GM3); the flat view beside it and the numbers are what a reader reads off exactly, since no one reads an angle off a turned picture.",
];

// Each space: its introduction, the measurement in words, what it is for here.

pub const CIRCLE: [&str; 3] = [
    "The circle is the simplest manifold: one dimension (an ant on it can go forward or back, nothing else) drawn in two. Its map is a single number, the angle round from the right-hand side. Along it, the distance between two points is the angle between them, in radians: the arc. Through the plane it is the chord, the string pulled straight.",
    "Two points a tenth of a radian apart: the chord and the arc agree to a part in two thousand. Two points two and a half radians apart (most of the way round to the opposite side): the arc is a third longer than the chord. Locally flat, and not flat.",
    "The sphere's one-dimensional case, and the picture to keep in mind for every space below. The functions that measure it are the sphere's: in X_eTaL the circle is the unit sphere in two dimensions, nothing more.",
];

pub const SPHERE: [&str; 4] = [
    "The unit sphere: every point one unit from the middle, two dimensions of surface (north and east) drawn in three. Its map is longitude and latitude, as on a globe, and the wire lines are the map's lines: parallels of constant latitude, meridians of constant longitude. Three points are placed by their longitude and latitude in degrees: p, q near it (six degrees east and three north), and r far from it. The distance along the sphere is the great-circle distance, the angle at the middle between the two points, in radians; the straight line is the chord through the inside.",
    "Worked in full, p to q. The chord: subtract q's three numbers from p's, square each difference, add, take the square root. The great circle: the program uses 2 atan2(|p - q|, |p + q|), the angle from the lengths of the difference and of the sum, which keeps its digits where the textbook arc-cosine of the dot product loses them for near points. The two distances differ by less than a part in two thousand. From p to r the great circle is longer than the chord by two thirds: the string from p to r runs deep through the ball.",
    "On the map the arcs are not straight: a great circle drawn on a flat map curves (airline routes do), because the map stretches the sphere near the poles. A map of a curved manifold is never to scale everywhere; the sphere's flat view is for reading positions, not lengths.",
    "The first representation this repository compares against flat space (the page representation-explorer, on data that has directions and no lengths), the space a flow is held to (dynamics-explorer), and the simplest manifold on which an optimizer's step can be kept on the manifold (optimization-explorer). sphere-hello, the command-line demo before this page, already shows the great circle and the chord ranking a point's neighbors the same way, and a mean that stays on the sphere where the plain average sinks inside it.",
];

pub const TORUS: [&str; 3] = [
    "The torus is the surface of a ring: two dimensions (round the hole, round the tube) drawn in three, with a hole, which no sphere has. Its map is a square of two angles, u round the hole and v round the tube, and the square's opposite edges are joined: walk off the top and you come in at the bottom. The torus drawn here has radii 3 (middle of the hole to middle of the tube) and 1 (the tube).",
    "The measurement here is of the map's honesty. The outer ring (v = 0) and the inner ring (v = 180 degrees) are the same line of the map, one square's width each. Measured along the wire, segment by segment, the outer is twice the inner: the map is stretched outside the ring and squeezed inside it. A step of the same size on the map is a longer step on the outside of the torus than on the inside, so distances cannot be read off the map; they must be measured along the surface. The sphere's map had the same fault near the poles; the Swiss roll's, below, has none.",
    "The space of two angles, which is what a pair of periodic quantities is (two phases, two directions of a compass, the two hands of a clock). A model that represents such a pair on a flat plane tears it at the seam; one that represents it on the torus does not. The torus appears here when a later page needs a periodic representation (plan, the Manifold library's flat torus); it is in the tour so that the idea of a map with joined edges is met early.",
];

pub const ROLL: [&str; 4] = [
    "The Swiss roll is a flat sheet rolled up: two dimensions (along the sheet, and up it) drawn in three. It is the classic picture of a data manifold, the curved thing a data set is supposed to lie near, used by every method that tries to unroll data since Isomap (2000). The program builds it from the sheet's own coordinates: t winds round the axis, farther out with every turn, and h goes straight up. Because the sheet is flat, its map is the sheet itself: the distance along the roll from t out to t is a formula, (t sqrt(1 + t squared) + asinh t) / 2, and the distance along the sheet between two points is plain Pythagoras in the unrolled coordinates.",
    "Three points: a, then b a little farther round, then c one full turn farther round at the same height, so that c sits directly above a's turn, one layer out. a to b: through the air and along the sheet agree to half a percent, locally flat again. a to c: through the air, two pi, the gap between layers; along the sheet, eight times as far.",
    "That is the trap for machine learning in one picture. A method that takes the nearest points through the room as neighbors takes c as a neighbor of a, when along the data they are far apart and everything between them lies on the sheet. Nearest neighbors, clustering and the distances a model is trained to respect all inherit the error. Unrolling the sheet (finding the map from the points) is what manifold learning means; this repository does not build an unroller, it asks what changes when the geometry is chosen well or badly.",
    "The data manifold against the representation manifold, two things the word manifold is used for and this repository keeps apart. The data sets of the experiment kit (flat clusters, directions, a tree) are each built to have a known structure for the same reason the roll has one: so that a comparison can say whether a geometry matched it.",
];

pub const DISK: [&str; 4] = [
    "The Poincare disk is the odd one out: it is drawn flat, in its own two dimensions, and it stays flat on the page because it cannot be drawn to scale in any room at all. It is the inside of the unit circle with a different ruler: the ruler shrinks toward the rim, so that a step near the rim that looks tiny on paper is long by the disk's measure, and the rim itself is infinitely far away (no point of the disk reaches it). It is a model of hyperbolic geometry, the geometry in which there is more room than on a flat plane: the circumference of a circle grows like an exponential of its radius, not like the radius.",
    "The disk's ruler is laid along a diameter: the marks are 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 apart by the disk's distance, and they bunch up toward the rim. The measurement: two points near the rim, 20 degrees apart round it, are a third of a unit apart on paper and nearly four units apart by the disk; the same two points drawn in toward the middle are twice as far by the disk as on paper, the disk's ruler at the middle being exactly twice the plain one. The geodesics, the shortest paths by the disk's distance, are drawn: a diameter is straight, and every other one is an arc of a circle that meets the rim at right angles, bowing toward the middle where the ruler is cheaper.",
    "The distance is 2 atanh of the length of q seen from p, p and q combined by the disk's own addition (Mobius addition); the geodesic is p plus a fraction of the arrow to q, the fraction taken the disk's way. The formulas work in any number of dimensions, where the disk becomes the Poincare ball; the library names them for the ball and this page uses them in two.",
    "Trees. A hierarchy (a taxonomy, a file system, the hypernyms of words) has a number of nodes that grows exponentially with depth, which no flat space of low dimension can hold at the right distances, and the disk can: the room grows exponentially with the radius. The page representation-explorer fits a tree into flat space, onto the sphere and into the disk at the same dimension and measures the distortion of each; that is the first hypothesis of this repository (H1: the geometry that matches the data's structure helps), tested with its null result written in advance.",
];

pub const SUMMARY: [&str; 4] = [
    "The question was what a manifold is, and why an experiment in machine learning would care. The answer, measured five times: a manifold is a space that is locally flat, and the measurement of that is a near pair of points whose straight-line distance and distance along the space agree (to a part in two thousand on the circle and on the sphere, half a percent on the roll), beside a far pair whose two distances do not (a third longer on the circle, two thirds on the sphere, eight times on the roll, twelve times on the disk near its rim).",
    "The experiments care because the straight line through the room is the default distance of every model, and on a manifold it is the wrong one for far pairs: it takes the point one layer out on the roll for a neighbor, it sinks the mean of points on a sphere into the ball, it has no room for a tree. Whether a geometry chosen to match the data helps a model learn is the question of the pages to come; this page only shows that the distances differ, and by how much.",
    "What the page established about its own machinery: an X_eTaL program of this repository, using its own library on top of the siblings' at pinned commits, runs in the browser, prints every coordinate and distance, and the page draws them, turning, with the map and the numbers beside. The turning view is the page's stopgap until the shared one lands.",
    "What it did not do: train, compare models, or claim that any geometry is better. Each of the three claims above (neighbors, the mean, trees) is a measurement of the pages that make it, not of this one.",
];

